[ti:The Most Mispronounced Words of 2023]
[by:VOA Learning English]

[00:01.33]News reporters, politicians and public figures have had trouble saying some of the words that relate to the top stories of the year.
[00:11.46]Last week, the online language-learning company Babbel released its lists of the most mispronounced words in the United States and Britain.
[00:22.58]The two lists are a reminder of the year's news on everything from scientific discoveries to politics.
[00:31.06]Babbel teacher Malcolm Massey noted the diversity of the words.
[00:36.56]This year's words come from several different languages.
[00:40.86]'I think a lot of it is due to how close our cultures have become because of how globalized things are,' Massey said.
[00:50.20]A popular singer who spells her name SZA is on the U.S. list.
[00:57.40]Babbel notes her name is pronounced 'Siza.'
[01:02.06]The first name of another entertainer is also on the list.
[01:06.98]Cillian Murphy is an Irish actor who starred in this summer's hit as physicist J. Robert Oppenheimer.
[01:15.88]His first name is pronounced 'KILL-ian.'
[01:20.38]Other pronunciations on the U.S. list include the name of biotech businessman Vivek Ramaswamy.
[01:28.30]He is a Republican presidential candidate.
[01:32.16]The correct way to say his name is Vivek Ramaswamy.
[01:37.63]Two volcanoes were also on the list: Mexico's Popocatepetl and Hawaii's Kilauea.
[01:46.30]The winning word at this year's Scripps National Spelling Bee also made the U.S. list.
[01:53.12]That word was psammophile.
[01:56.73]It means any animal or plant that prefers sandy soils or areas.
[02:02.62]The coronation of King Charles in May helped to put the Stone of Scone on Britain's list.
[02:09.00]The holy stone rests beneath the coronation chair where a new king first receives his crown.
[02:18.20]Christy Denlinger teaches linguistics at the University of Texas at Austin.
[02:24.41]She said hearing an unfamiliar word often enough can help someone master it.
[02:31.69]'Anything that is kind of different from what a speaker is used to, they can learn how to pronounce things in different ways than what they're used to.'
[02:40.78]It just takes more exposure, Denlinger said.
[02:45.51]Massey said that as one learns a new pronunciation, it is important not to stress perfection, but progress over time.
[02:55.25]So, practicing these words again and again.
[02:59.50]In some cases, the correct pronunciation is just a matter of personal choice.
[03:05.96]Earning a place on the U.S. list was the last name of Travis Kelce.
[03:11.92]The Kansas City Chiefs American football player.
[03:16.27]Whether Kelce should be pronounced with one or two syllables.
[03:21.46]Kells or Kelce has been a subject even his own family talks about.
[03:28.48]Both Travis and his brother, Philadelphia Eagles center Jason Kelce, say their last name as Kelce.
[03:37.06]On a podcast, they discussed with their father, Ed Kelce, how that pronunciation came to be.
[03:44.58]'I got tired of correcting people,' said Ed Kelce, who said that his coworkers always called him Kelce.
[03:53.74]Jason Kelce then asked, 'Should we go by Kells or Kelce?'
[03:59.80]His father answers:
[04:01.88]'Do whatever you want.'
[04:04.02]'I did.'
[04:05.54]I'm Jill Robbins.
